cl inlines the member functions of these local classes away and emits no symbol for them, where cxx1 emits a real function - so cxx1's object has ?get@L@?1??f@@YAHXZ@QEAAHXZ and the other two and cl's has none of them. It is the same difference docs/CONFORMANCE.md records as "An inline member function is a strong symbol": cxx1 has no COMDAT and no way to say a definition is mergeable, so it emits one and cl is free to emit none. The names themselves are not in question - tests/names.sh compares them against clang and they agree on all three targets.
